Setting up the Raspberry Pi

Installing the OS

    1. Configuring SD:
        a. Using the RPI Imager install Raspberry Pi OS Lite. We're using this because it's a faster install and doesn't require GUI. 
        b. In the custom settings we need to generate names that make sense so we can keept track of what's what.
            i. For the Source:
                a. Name/User/Password: source/source/source
            ii. For the Noisy Channel X:
                b. Name/User/Password: noisex/noisex/noisex

Setting Up The Directories

    1. Transferring Files:
        a. I find the easiest way is to use the SSH protocol built on mac.
            i. SSH to the RPI: run ssh name@user.local. Enter password when prompted.
        b. Enable SSH. Run sudo raspi-config. Under Interface Options, enable SSH and exit.
        c. Transferring Mac -> Pi:
            i. Run scp /path/on/mac/file.txt name@user.local:~/filepath
        d. Transferring Pi -> Mac:
            i. Run scp name@user.local:~/filepath .  <- PERIOD IS REQUIRED, COPIES FILE TO CURRENT DIR ON MAC
        e. To copy entire folder, modify to run scp -r //filepath <-RUNS RECURSIVELY OVER FOLDER

    2. Run on Boot:
        a. Now that we have the files available on the RPI, we need to make it run on boot since we're not going to be actually connecting to it. 

        b. Create a service routine to run the loop control. 
        Run the following: sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/tone_loop.service

        c. Within this window, copy the text from BootRun.txt and save. In the EX, the RPI is named source. This needs to be changed depending on which RPI is connected.

        d. Run the following commands:

        sudo systemctl daemon-reload
        sudo systemctl enable tone_loop.service
        sudo systemctl start tone_loop.service
        sudo systemctl status tone_loop.service

        e. Now, assuming the last line shows the status as active/enabled, then the loop control will run on boot after the OS is running. Verify this against the checking service enabled.png file

    


    


